From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 05:32:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 11216/11362] [outer-loop] Rolled back the model change after a
 2h conductor outage I caused

The conductor could not advance a milestone for about one hour fifty minutes.
Two independent tiers, audit and planner, each failed on their first invocation
after the 05:21Z model change and never succeeded since; the audit last wrote
its receipt at 03:24Z, before the change. BLOCK went 3 to 9 today against a 1-2
range for the week, which is what made it worth chasing.

Rolled back to gpt-5.6-sol. The drop-in pins the tiers explicitly rather than
being deleted, so the attempt and its outcome stay in the record.

Causation is not proven and the record says so. The logged error text is
truncated and its own producer's docstring says that field has historically
shown an echoed prompt tail rather than a real error; I nearly rolled back on
that string before reading the function. A direct probe of gpt-6-astra shows no
metadata warning. A 60KB-prompt test hung, and the identical test hung
identically on gpt-5.6-sol, so it was an argv artifact rather than a model
difference. That control is the only reason a wrong conclusion was not written.

What justifies the rollback is the empirical record, not a diagnosis. The
success criterion is stated in advance: the audit receipt should be rewritten
and a plan should succeed. If failures continue on the old model then the model
was not the cause and this rollback is itself a false conclusion.

The lesson is larger than the incident. I verified the model with two short
probes, shipped it to three production tiers, correctly recorded that nothing
had exercised them and that the next milestone close was the real test, and
then did not go back at the close. The watch item was recorded and not watched.
A capability probe is not a deployment test.

